] :activity/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124751}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488313} :activity/id #uuid "382bc946-87c1-5af9-a714-bd8312897100" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050470 :topic/id #uuid "4c435cda-6bf3-50f8-af96-25002a1726eb" :topic/math-academy-id 1966} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488290 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362257 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4ccb0fb-57b3-54cc-9a0e-7b3b2ec086c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452491} {:answer/id #uuid "bb0736bf-0992-5540-92e4-4db3dc860d7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452492}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452491} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7a2ad23-7a6f-5d92-beff-9522a7a1c6e4" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453093} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4c9d99e-8bcf-530d-a35b-845c74343724" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452493} {:answer/id #uuid "b94d0759-6f74-5338-954d-fc2d4ed8aa4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452494}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452494} :answer-field/id #uuid "eabb1107-ac4d-5f76-9ad8-0708cf4bdbd2" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453094} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aee5ed75-acac-56ef-98ed-57d5822de8c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452495} {:answer/id #uuid "537989db-6a16-5fcc-aa37-3d4aa93c947c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452496}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452496} :answer-field/id #uuid "86f83e84-79e0-5f94-a3f0-7b675d59f092" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453095}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "68bc418d-6421-5050-b933-2a80a591867e" :question/math-academy-id "q-97975" :question/problem "If $C$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(C+3I)=2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{Null}(C+3I)={0}$\n- **II.** $C+3I$ is singular\n- **III.** $\\lambda =-3$ is an eigenvalue of $C$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $4$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(C+3I) & =4-\\text{rank}(C+3I) \\\\  & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\text{Null}(C+3I)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(C+3I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $C-3I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=C+3I$ is singular, then $(C+3I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $C.$ As a result, $\\lambda =-3$ is an eigenvalue of $C.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "0a855166-560a-5030-8b45-18d477914230" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488301}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488301 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488246 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dd0d4fb-280a-550e-a592-ead821179211" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487590} {:answer/id #uuid "1e87bc2e-1fa6-5dfe-a12d-f3281ca37a48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487628} {:answer/id #uuid "79c00a2a-a33d-50c2-9a06-fe7d080d7095" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487806} {:answer/id #uuid "89763caf-f10d-5fe3-91a2-c8336bf4bcdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487830} {:answer/id #uuid "ff51444c-16eb-5add-9d3a-db4575d4b382" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488049}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487628} :answer-field/id #uuid "93a75aae-536a-560a-b748-38762d5ef8b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a57f1e60-3a1f-5809-920d-a2e3527a6835" :question/math-academy-id "q-140839" :question/problem "If $C$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\lambda =5$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $(C-5I)x=0$ has only zero solution\n\nII. $\\text{Col}(C-5I)\\ne {R}^{4}$\n\nIII. $\\text{Null}(C-5I)\\ne \\{0\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=C-5I.$\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that if $\\lambda =5$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(C-5I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $C.$ As a result, the matrix $M=C-5I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Since $M=C-5I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(C-5I)\\ne {R}^{4},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(C-5I)\\ne \\{0\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "44b1d683-1553-59db-aadd-0a5e6069911a"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488313 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362248 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "319126a6-cb98-5c29-8c30-bfd20133ec43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452443} {:answer/id #uuid "285ae59e-5ddd-5dc9-862d-3bc834ee97db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452444}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452444} :answer-field/id #uuid "cff45205-5a50-56b9-95a8-9585e7c8f98c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453069} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "632c7bfe-66b2-5e98-9e93-398f5b32a709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452445} {:answer/id #uuid "abe36428-fb32-540e-8f8d-034368a9e822" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452446}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452445} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5451bbf-2703-59ef-ad8f-b44789b95918" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453070} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3935095-5efb-57a9-a398-831d61d53049" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452447} {:answer/id #uuid "c29be87d-ebc5-57fb-9a6d-2a010829060a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452448}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452447} :answer-field/id #uuid "78acad20-e495-541a-94b6-43117568da9f" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453071}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b1b3df2e-4878-5f07-9682-4c09eb922bca" :question/math-academy-id "q-97945" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(A+I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution\n- **II.** $\\text{rank}(A+I)=3$\n- **III.** $\\text{Null}(A+I)={0}$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=A+I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A+I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, the matrix $M=A+I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are false. Since $M=A+I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A+I)\\ne 3,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A+I)\\ne {0}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "6a18fb35-57db-5780-914c-83b1dbef88e1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488316}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488316 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362252 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9837a523-42e0-533a-a0dd-b42b2ad407d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452461} {:answer/id #uuid "7952bb3e-1500-544d-a00b-8d59f4c87d8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 