m(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=C-I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =1$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(C-I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $C.$ As a result, the matrix $M=C-I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $M=C-I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(C-I)\\ne 2.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $M=C-I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(C-I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "901bfe44-d04a-5966-874a-7a238c9dc1ba" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488346}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488346 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362254 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2cedf5f-ea78-598b-8fea-86eb4930a16e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452473} {:answer/id #uuid "ac501363-d562-542c-8971-fa619b64fb93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452474}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452474} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ed2e22e-171c-554d-a637-c726d3aaa309" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453084} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "941dacde-a17c-5765-910f-5bc4839a1386" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452475} {:answer/id #uuid "5f487188-0cec-5f38-9e34-e190f507bb5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452476}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452476} :answer-field/id #uuid "611e09b5-b47a-5519-9300-6bc0ece8a2f0" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453085} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0044e830-172d-574e-b097-e1ebe25e1adb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452477} {:answer/id #uuid "4200c14b-bbe6-5725-adaa-903da877df9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452478}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452477} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ef6a8d9-228c-5e0b-8e7a-462c8a409290" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453086}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "651bcec7-c2ce-52d1-b1e2-d8f7e6384008" :question/math-academy-id "q-97969" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(A-3I)=4,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{nullity}(A-3I)=0$\n- **II.** $A-3I$ is invertible\n- **III.** $\\lambda =3$ is an eigenvalue of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $4$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(A-3I) & =4-\\text{rank}(A-3I) \\\\  & =4-4 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A-3I)=0,$ we obtain that $A-3I$ must be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that if $M=A-3I$ is invertible, then $(A-3I)x=0$ has only zero solution. As a result, $\\lambda =3$ can not be an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "f5e9d767-80a5-5062-a420-ab68984dcbd8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488290}}] :activity/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487555}]}
